{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) is the nerve of the second branchial arch. It is mainly motor to the muscles of facial expression, and also carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, secretomotor fibres to the lacrimal and salivary glands, and fibres to stapedius. It leaves the skull at the stylomastoid foramen and divides in the parotid gland into five branches.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## Why the facial nerve matters

The facial nerve is the cranial nerve most often involved in functional deficit. Its course is long and complex. It starts inside the skull, runs in a non-expandable bony canal, then passes through the parotid gland and the superficial tissues of the face. That is why trauma, inflammation and tumours can all damage it. The motor part is the largest part of the nerve, and it causes the most disability when the nerve is injured.

## Components of the nerve

The facial nerve is a **mixed nerve**. It has a motor root and a sensory root. The sensory root is called the **nervus intermedius** (nerve of Wrisberg). It carries sensory and parasympathetic fibres and runs with the motor root. Each nerve has about 10 000 fibres, two-thirds motor and one-third sensory.

{% table %}
- **Component**
- **What it supplies or carries**
---
- **Special visceral efferent (motor root)**
- All muscles derived from the second branchial arch: the muscles of facial expression, the auricular muscles (now vestigial), stylohyoid, the posterior belly of digastric and stapedius
---
- **General visceral efferent (parasympathetic)**
- Secretomotor fibres to the lacrimal, submandibular and sublingual glands, and to the smaller glands of the nasal mucosa and palate
---
- **Special visceral afferent (taste)**
- Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue (through the chorda tympani) and from the palate (through the greater superficial petrosal nerve). Taste goes to the nucleus of the tractus solitarius.
---
- **General somatic afferent**
- General sensation from the concha, the posterosuperior part of the external canal and the tympanic membrane. This explains the vesicular eruption seen in herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion.
{% /table %}

Three points are often asked:

- The facial nerve passes through the parotid gland but does **not** supply it. The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) does.
- The **anterior** belly of digastric is supplied by the trigeminal nerve (CN V). The facial nerve supplies the **posterior** belly.
- Taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue is carried by CN IX, not CN VII.

The facial nerve does not supply the skin of the face. Its branches communicate with branches of the trigeminal nerve. Proprioceptive fibres from the facial muscles are believed to leave in these communications and travel to the central nervous system in the trigeminal nerve.

## Nuclei and central connections

- The **facial motor nucleus** lies in the lower pons, in the caudal pontine reticular formation. Neurones for the scalp and upper face are dorsal. Neurones for the lower facial muscles are ventral.
- The **superior salivatory nucleus** in the pons gives the parasympathetic fibres.
- Taste fibres end in the **nucleus of the tractus solitarius**.

**Supranuclear (upper motor neuron) supply:** corticobulbar fibres run from the precentral gyrus to the internal capsule, then to the lower brainstem, and end in the facial nucleus in the pons. The part of the nucleus for the upper face receives fibres from **both** cerebral cortices. The part for the lower face is supplied mainly from the opposite cortex. The nuclei also receive extrapyramidal, basal ganglia and hypothalamic input that is concerned with facial muscle tone and with automatic and emotional movements.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Remember the face in two halves:** the upper face (frontalis) has bilateral cortical supply, so it is spared in an upper motor neuron lesion. The lower face has mainly contralateral supply, so it is weak on the side opposite the lesion.
{% /callout %}

## Course of the facial nerve

**1. In the brainstem.** Axons of the motor neurones first run dorsomedially towards the fourth ventricle. They loop around the abducent (CN VI) nucleus, then descend ventrolaterally between the facial nucleus and the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve. The bump on the dorsal pons made by this loop is the **facial colliculus**. The nerve emerges between the olive and the restiform body at the **cerebellopontine angle**.

**2. Cerebellopontine angle and internal acoustic meatus.** The motor and sensory roots cross the cerebellopontine angle and enter the internal acoustic meatus with the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII). In the meatus the nerve has no fibrous sheath or endoneurium and is surrounded by a thin layer of arachnoid. The motor root and nervus intermedius usually merge here.

**3. Facial (fallopian) canal in the temporal bone.** The course through the temporal bone is about 3 cm long. It has three segments. A **genu** is a bend in the nerve.

{% table %}
- **Segment**
- **Key points**
---
- **Labyrinthine**
- From the fundus of the internal acoustic meatus to the geniculate ganglion. It is the shortest and narrowest part of the nerve and lacks anastomosing arterial cascades, so it is prone to vascular compression. At its end is the first genu, where the **geniculate ganglion** lies.
---
- **Tympanic (horizontal)**
- About 1 cm long, on the medial wall of the middle ear. It lies medial to the cochleariform process, above the oval window and below the lateral semicircular canal. It ends at the second genu, a turn of about 90 degrees just beyond the pyramidal eminence.
---
- **Mastoid (vertical)**
- Descends from the second genu to the stylomastoid foramen. It gives the nerve to stapedius and, about 6 mm before the exit, the chorda tympani.
{% /table %}

The tight bony canal makes the nerve vulnerable to inflammation and oedema. In patients with Bell's palsy, the affected side usually matches the side with the narrower facial canal on CT.

The fallopian canal may be dehiscent (have a gap in its bony wall) in up to 50 per cent of cases, especially when congenital ear malformations are present.

**4. Outside the skull.** The nerve leaves through the **stylomastoid foramen**. It gives off the posterior auricular nerve and the nerves to the posterior belly of digastric and stylohyoid almost at once. It then enters the parotid gland on its posteromedial surface and divides into an upper **temporofacial** trunk and a lower **cervicofacial** trunk. These form the **parotid plexus (pes anserinus, "goose foot")**, which gives five main terminal branches.

## Branches within the temporal bone

{% table %}
- **Branch**
- **Where it arises**
- **What it does**
---
- **Greater (superficial) petrosal nerve**
- Geniculate ganglion
- Carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibres to the pterygopalatine (sphenopalatine) ganglion. After the relay, fibres go to the lacrimal gland and to the glands of the nose and palate. It may also carry taste fibres from the palate.
---
- **Nerve to stapedius**
- Distal tympanic or upper mastoid segment
- Motor to stapedius
---
- **Chorda tympani**
- Mastoid segment, about 6 mm before the exit
- Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and preganglionic parasympathetic fibres to the submandibular ganglion
{% /table %}

The **geniculate ganglion** is a sensory ganglion. The taste fibres in the nerve are peripheral processes of its pseudounipolar neurones.

**Chorda tympani pathway:** it leaves through the petrotympanic fissure into the infratemporal fossa and joins the lingual nerve. Its parasympathetic fibres relay in the submandibular ganglion. Postganglionic fibres supply the submandibular and sublingual glands and the mucous membrane of the mouth and tongue.

**Greater petrosal pathway:** it joins the deep petrosal nerve (sympathetic) to form the nerve of the pterygoid canal, which runs to the pterygopalatine ganglion. This pathway is why the dry eye of Bell's palsy occurs.

## Terminal branches and the muscles they supply

{% table %}
- **Branch**
- **Course**
- **Muscles supplied**
---
- **Temporal**
- Leaves the upper border of the parotid and crosses the zygomatic arch
- Anterior and superior auricular muscles, intrinsic muscles on the lateral side of the ear, frontalis (frontal belly of occipitofrontalis), upper orbicularis oculi, corrugator supercilii
---
- **Zygomatic**
- Crosses the zygomatic bone to the lateral canthus
- Orbicularis oculi (lower part)
---
- **Buccal**
- Leaves the anterior border of the parotid and lies close to the parotid duct
- Buccinator, muscles of the upper lip and nostril, orbicularis oris
---
- **Marginal mandibular**
- Runs below the angle of the mandible, deep to platysma, then crosses the body of the mandible
- Risorius, muscles of the lower lip and chin, depressor anguli oris
---
- **Cervical**
- Emerges from the lower part of the parotid and runs forward in the neck under platysma
- Platysma
{% /table %}

The muscles of facial expression produce all voluntary and involuntary movements of the face, except those of the jaws. The only muscle of the group not supplied by the facial nerve is levator palpebrae superioris.

**Anastomoses between branches** are common, and the branching pattern varies a good deal between people. This probably explains why accidental or deliberate division of a small branch often fails to cause the weakness you would expect.

## Blood supply

The nerve is supplied by four vessels, each for a different stretch:

1. **Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA):** the nerve in the cerebellopontine angle
2. **Labyrinthine artery** (a branch of AICA): the nerve in the internal acoustic meatus
3. **Superficial petrosal artery** (a branch of the middle meningeal artery): the geniculate ganglion and the region next to it
4. **Stylomastoid artery** (a branch of the posterior auricular artery): the mastoid and tympanic segments

All of these form an **external plexus** in the epineurium, which feeds a deeper **internal plexus** inside the nerve. The intrapetrous part, especially the labyrinthine segment, is less well vascularised than the rest, which fits with its vulnerability.

## Surgical landmarks

Finding the nerve safely matters in middle ear, mastoid and parotid surgery.

{% table %}
- **Surgery**
- **Landmark**
- **Relation of the nerve**
---
- **Middle ear and mastoid**
- Processus cochleariformis
- Marks the geniculate ganglion, which lies just in front of it. The tympanic segment starts here.
---
- **Middle ear and mastoid**
- Oval window and horizontal canal
- The nerve runs above the oval window and below the horizontal (lateral semicircular) canal
---
- **Middle ear and mastoid**
- Short process of incus
- The nerve lies medial to it, at the level of the aditus
---
- **Middle ear and mastoid**
- Pyramid
- The nerve runs behind the pyramid
---
- **Middle ear and mastoid**
- Tympanomastoid suture
- In the mastoid segment, the nerve runs behind this suture
---
- **Middle ear and mastoid**
- Digastric ridge
- The nerve leaves the mastoid at the anterior end of the ridge
---
- **Parotid**
- Tragal (cartilaginous) pointer
- The nerve lies about 1 cm deep to it, slightly inferior and anterior
---
- **Parotid**
- Tympanomastoid suture
- The nerve lies 6 to 8 mm deep to the suture
---
- **Parotid**
- Styloid process
- The nerve crosses lateral (superficial) to it
---
- **Parotid**
- Posterior belly of digastric
- Trace the upper border backwards to the digastric groove. The nerve lies between the muscle and the styloid process, just above the muscle where it meets the mastoid.
{% /table %}

When the anatomy is altered, for example in revision cases or after radiotherapy, a **retrograde technique** can be used. The surgeon finds a main branch (the buccal branch near the parotid duct, the marginal mandibular branch near the facial vessels, or the temporal branch) and follows it back to the trunk.

In the extratemporal course the nerve can be injured by facial trauma, or during surgery on the parotid or submandibular glands, the temporomandibular joint, or facelift procedures. The temporofacial division crosses the zygoma about 1 cm in front of the ear, where it is vulnerable. The marginal mandibular branch has an important relation to the lower border of the mandible.

## Development

The facial nerve is the nerve of the **second branchial arch**. Development begins at 3 weeks of gestation. The nerve is fully developed about 4 years after birth. Because the first arch gives rise to parts of the external and middle ear, facial nerve anomalies should be expected when the external or middle ear is malformed. At birth the stylomastoid foramen is more superficial, because the mastoid tip is still developing.

## Examining the facial nerve

Ask the patient to:

- Raise the eyebrows and look for symmetrical wrinkling of the forehead (frontalis)
- Screw the eyes tightly shut while you try to open them (orbicularis oculi)
- Bare the teeth (orbicularis oris)
- Blow out the cheeks with the mouth closed (buccinator and orbicularis oris)

Taste is rarely tested. Minor facial asymmetry is common and is rarely pathological.

## Upper versus lower motor neuron facial palsy

An **upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion** is a lesion above the facial nucleus. A **lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion** is a lesion of the nucleus or of the nerve beyond it.

{% table %}
- **Feature**
- **UMN lesion**
- **LMN lesion**
---
- **Side of weakness**
- Opposite side to the lesion
- Same side as the lesion
---
- **Forehead (frontalis)**
- Spared, because of bilateral supply. Brow furrowing is preserved.
- Weak, so frowning and forehead wrinkling are lost
---
- **Eye closure**
- Largely unaffected
- Weak. Corneal exposure and ulceration occur if the eye does not close in sleep.
---
- **Lower face**
- Weak. The earliest sign is slowing of one side of the face, for example on baring the teeth.
- The angle of the mouth falls and there is dribbling
---
- **Emotional versus voluntary movement**
- Spontaneous emotional movement (smiling) may be relatively preserved
- Weak on both voluntary and spontaneous movement, with no dissociation
---
- **Taste**
- Not affected
- Frequently impaired, if the lesion is above the chorda tympani
{% /table %}

In a peripheral palsy, the involved eye does not blink whichever side is stimulated in the **corneal reflex**. The afferent limb is the trigeminal nerve and the efferent limb is the facial nerve. With a severe lesion, the muscles may atrophy over time.

## Localising a lower motor neuron lesion

The associated findings point to the level of the lesion.

{% table %}
- **Site of lesion**
- **Associated findings**
---
- **Supranuclear (cortex, internal capsule, midbrain)**
- Contralateral paralysis of the lower face with relative sparing of the upper face
---
- **Facial nucleus**
- No localising signs, but the abducent (VI) nerve may be involved. Often bilateral. Causes include motor neuron disease and neoplasm.
---
- **Intrapontine fibres**
- Ipsilateral CN VI or lateral gaze palsy and, with a ventral pontine lesion, contralateral hemiplegia (the Millard-Gubler pattern). Taste is normal.
---
- **Cerebellopontine angle**
- Deafness, tinnitus and vertigo (CN VIII), facial sensory loss (CN V), loss of taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, reduced tear and salivary secretion, and hyperacusis. Acoustic neuroma is a notable cause.
---
- **Facial canal**
- Only CN VII is involved. Findings depend on the level (see tests below).
---
- **Outside the temporal bone (parotid area)**
- Motor loss only. It may be incomplete, because tumour or trauma may spare some branches.
{% /table %}

**Topodiagnostic tests** find the site of a lesion in the canal:

- **Schirmer test:** compares tear flow on the two sides. Reduced tears mean the lesion is proximal to the geniculate ganglion, because secretomotor fibres leave there in the greater petrosal nerve.
- **Stapedial reflex:** lost when the lesion is above the nerve to stapedius. It is tested by tympanometry.
- **Taste test or electrogustometry:** impaired taste means the lesion is above the chorda tympani.
- **Submandibular salivary flow:** reduced flow also shows injury above the chorda tympani.

Some current practice does not do these tests routinely, because they have limited prognostic value. Electrical tests (electroneuronography and electromyography) are now used more to judge the degree of dysfunction and the chance of recovery.

**Hyperacusis** means decreased tolerance of loud noises. It occurs in the ipsilateral ear when stapedius is paralysed, so low tones sound louder and higher.

## Bell's palsy and other causes of facial palsy

**Bell's palsy** is the name for an idiopathic lower motor neuron facial palsy. It may be complete or partial. Features are:

- Flaccid paralysis of the muscles of facial expression on the same side
- Decreased tear production on that side (greater petrosal nerve)
- Hyperacusis (stapedius paralysis)

The cause remains uncertain. MRI suggests inflammation of the labyrinthine and perigeniculate segments. In the vast majority of cases, full recovery occurs spontaneously after a few weeks.

**Management points:**

- **Protect the eye.** Use lubricating drops through the day and a thicker lubricant at night, with taping of the eye shut at night.
- **Corticosteroids** (prednisolone) have a good evidence base, especially if started early. Doses differ between references, so follow your local protocol and check for contraindications such as pregnancy, diabetes, hypertension, peptic ulcer, pulmonary tuberculosis and glaucoma.
- **Antivirals** alone are of questionable value. Vesicular lesions in the palate or external auditory canal are the case where they should be used.
- **Surgical decompression** has no evidence of benefit and may be harmful.
- **Imaging** is needed if loss progresses over weeks, recovery is not seen in 3 weeks, or there is twitching, other cranial nerve deficits or recurrence. A tumour must be ruled out.

**Other causes:**

- **Ramsay Hunt syndrome:** herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion
- Tumours of the parotid and of the temporal bone
- Temporal bone fractures. Gunshot injuries cause facial paralysis more often than closed head injury.
- Surgery: in middle ear surgery, the tympanic segment is the commonest site of injury. The nerve can also be damaged in the cerebellopontine angle during acoustic neuroma surgery, or during parotid surgery.

**Hemifacial spasm** is painless, irregular, involuntary contraction of one side of the face. Most cases are related to vascular compression of the nerve as it exits the pons.

## Grading and injury classification

The **House-Brackmann score** measures facial motor weakness. It has six grades, from normal (grade I) to total paralysis (grade VI). Grade IV has no forehead function and incomplete eye closure.

Nerve injury is classed in five degrees, and the prognosis falls as the degree rises:

{% table %}
- **Degree**
- **Injury**
- **Prognosis**
---
- **First**
- Conduction block, no anatomical disruption (neurapraxia)
- Reversible, full recovery
---
- **Second**
- Division of the axon (axonotmesis)
- Reasonable recovery
---
- **Third**
- Division of axon and endoneurium
- Incomplete recovery
---
- **Fourth**
- Complete transection with epineurium intact (neurotmesis)
- Poor recovery
---
- **Fifth**
- Complete transection of the nerve
- No recovery
{% /table %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The part of the facial nucleus for the upper face receives corticobulbar fibres from both cerebral cortices. An upper motor neuron lesion therefore spares the forehead and weakens the opposite lower face." %}
In an upper motor neuron lesion of the facial nerve, the forehead is spared because:
- A. Frontalis is supplied by the trigeminal nerve
- B. The lesion is on the same side as the weakness
- C. The upper face receives bilateral supranuclear innervation
- D. The temporal branch leaves the nerve before the lesion
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Secretomotor fibres to the lacrimal gland leave at the geniculate ganglion in the greater petrosal nerve. Reduced tear flow on the Schirmer test therefore places the lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion." %}
A patient with a lower motor neuron facial palsy shows reduced tear flow on the Schirmer test. The lesion must be:
- A. Distal to the chorda tympani
- B. Proximal to the geniculate ganglion
- C. At the stylomastoid foramen
- D. In the parotid gland
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The facial nerve passes through the parotid gland but does not supply it. The parotid receives its secretomotor supply from the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)." %}
Which of the following is NOT supplied by the facial nerve?
- A. Stapedius
- B. Posterior belly of digastric
- C. Sublingual gland
- D. Parotid gland
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The nerve to stapedius arises from the distal tympanic or upper mastoid segment. Paralysis of stapedius lets low tones sound louder, which is hyperacusis. Tensor tympani is supplied by the trigeminal system, not the facial nerve." %}
Hyperacusis in facial nerve palsy is due to paralysis of:
- A. Stapedius
- B. Tensor tympani
- C. Buccinator
- D. Platysma
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The labyrinthine segment is the shortest and narrowest part of the nerve in its canal and lacks anastomosing arterial cascades, which makes it susceptible to vascular compression." %}
Which segment of the facial nerve in the temporal bone is most susceptible to vascular compression?
- A. Mastoid segment
- B. Labyrinthine segment
- C. Tympanic segment
- D. Meatal segment
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="In the pons, the facial fibres loop around the abducent nucleus. A nuclear or intrapontine lesion can therefore cause ipsilateral facial weakness together with a lateral rectus (VI) palsy, and a ventral pontine lesion adds contralateral hemiplegia." %}
Ipsilateral facial weakness with an ipsilateral lateral rectus palsy and contralateral hemiplegia suggests a lesion in the:
- A. Cerebellopontine angle
- B. Internal capsule
- C. Pons
- D. Parotid gland
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What are the components of the facial nerve?" %}
Special visceral efferent to the muscles of the second branchial arch, general visceral efferent (secretomotor to lacrimal, submandibular and sublingual glands and nasal and palatal glands), special visceral afferent (taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and the palate), and general somatic afferent (concha, external canal, tympanic membrane).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where does the facial nerve leave the skull and what are its first branches?" %}
Through the stylomastoid foramen. The posterior auricular nerve and the nerves to the posterior belly of digastric and stylohyoid arise almost at once. The nerve then enters the parotid gland.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the five terminal branches." %}
Temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular and cervical. They arise from the parotid plexus (pes anserinus) after the nerve divides into temporofacial and cervicofacial trunks.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which branches arise inside the temporal bone?" %}
The greater petrosal nerve from the geniculate ganglion, the nerve to stapedius, and the chorda tympani from the mastoid segment about 6 mm before the stylomastoid foramen.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What does the chorda tympani carry?" %}
Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and preganglionic parasympathetic fibres to the submandibular ganglion, for the submandibular and sublingual glands. It joins the lingual nerve.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How do you tell a UMN from an LMN facial palsy?" %}
In a UMN lesion the opposite lower face is weak and the forehead is spared, because the upper face has bilateral supranuclear supply. In an LMN lesion all muscles on the same side are weak, including frontalis and eye closure.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How can you find the facial nerve trunk during parotid surgery?" %}
It lies about 1 cm deep to the tip of the tragal pointer. It is also found 6 to 8 mm deep to the tympanomastoid suture, crossing lateral to the styloid process, and just above the posterior belly of digastric where it meets the mastoid.
{% /viva %}

